@page "/exgrid"
@using ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid

<DocsPage Product="ExGrid" Title="Overview"
          Lead="A grid for reading large tables of numbers, with a spreadsheet user's selection, keyboard and clipboard. Your application holds the rows; ExGrid shows them and tells you what the user asked for.">

    <DocsSection Title="A first grid">
        <p>
            Hand the grid a source and a list of columns. <code>GridSource.From</code> is the bundled
            in-memory source: it sorts and filters a list you already hold, and its behaviour is the
            reference for what sorting and filtering mean. Click a cell, drag across a rectangle, press
            the arrows, or open a column's menu from the ▾ in its header.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(FirstGrid)" Mud="typeof(FirstGridMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
        <p>
            Place <code>&lt;ExGridStylesheet /&gt;</code> in your head, as <a href="getting-started#link-the-stylesheets">Installation</a>
            shows; the grid loads its own script. With MudBlazor, add ExGrid.MudBlazor and
            <code>&lt;MudExGridStylesheet /&gt;</code> as well, and put the grid on a <code>MudExGridPaper</code> with
            <code>MudGridChrome</code>, as the MudBlazor tab of the code shows.
            ExGrid runs in Chrome and Edge, in an interactive render mode, on .NET 10 or newer.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A responsive box">
        <p>
            Set <code>ViewportWidth="ViewportSize.Stretch"</code> to follow the container's width,
            including a flex row or a CSS grid track. A Drawer or split pane can change that box without
            changing the browser window. A declared pixel width stays declared. Give each enclosing
            flex or grid item permission to shrink with <code>min-width: 0</code>; the grid and
            <code>MudExGridPaper</code> handle their own roots.
        </p>
        <p>
            Narrowing keeps the columns, their widths, their order and Density. Scroll horizontally
            to reach the rest. Pinning is temporarily suspended when it leaves less than 40 pixels
            for scrolling, then restored on widening without changing saved View State. Columns
            narrower than the box leave empty space on the right. This is responsive sizing; the
            complete mobile touch workflow is still being implemented.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Title="Resize the container" Builtin="typeof(ResponsiveBox)" Mud="typeof(ResponsiveBoxMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])">
            Narrow and widen this container. The first two columns stay the same width, and the
            rightmost column remains reachable through the horizontal scrollbar.
        </DocsExample>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A grid drawn in the browser">
        <p>
            Blazor's ExGrid is moving onto the same TypeScript core as React's, after which only the rows, their
            order and what an intent does to them stay in .NET. <code>ExGrid.Script.ExGrid</code> is that grid,
            beside the one above while the move is under way: the keys, the Selection, the clipboard and the
            editor run in the browser on both hosting models, so on Blazor Server a key or a drag no longer
            crosses the circuit. It takes the same <code>GridColumn</code>s and <code>IGridSource</code>, and its
            parameters and events are named as the core's are. Edit a Book below: the commit waits for
            <code>OnEdit</code> to answer, and the cell then paints the row your source holds. A column's
            <code>Validate</code>, a <code>Refuse</code> from <code>OnEdit</code>, <code>OnPaste</code> or
            <code>OnFill</code>, and <code>CellState</code> and <code>CellMessageOf</code> work as on the grid
            above; each answer crosses from .NET, and the input behind it waits for it
            (<a href="exgrid/editing#validation">a verdict may come later</a>). Select a few Books and drag the
            small square at the Selection's bottom-right down or up, or select a Trader alone and drag it left onto
            Book: with
            <code>ShowFillHandle</code> set, the release reaches <code>OnFill</code> as the same
            <code>GridFillIntent</code>, and once your handler completes without refusing, the Selection is the
            source and the target together.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ScriptCoreGrid)" Mud="typeof(ScriptCoreGridMud)" Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" />
        <p>
            Its <code>Chrome</code> takes the same <code>IGridChrome</code>, and a part your Chrome draws in Razor
            stands inside the grid the browser draws: the component renders it beside the grid, in a hidden holder,
            and the grid's script moves it into its place. <code>MudGridChrome</code> draws the column menu, the
            filter panel, the find panel, the Context Menu, the loading bar, the Cell Editor, the Formula Bar's two
            fields and the completion list this way, with the same MudBlazor buttons, checkboxes, fields, bar and list
            and the same underlined field; switch the Example to MudBlazor and open a column's ▾, press
            <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>, or type on a Book, in its cell or in the Formula Bar, which
            <code>ShowFormulaBar</code> stands above the header: <code>CompleteEditorText</code> offers the books whose
            names begin with what you typed, asked of .NET as you type, and ↑, ↓, Tab and a click on a book choose and
            accept one. The other parts are still the
            browser's own here, until the Wrapper draws them too. A Chrome of your own names the parts it draws in
            <code>ScriptSlots</code> and answers each through the overload that takes a view:
            <code>ColumnMenu(GridMenuView)</code>, <code>ContextMenu(GridMenuView)</code>,
            <code>FilterPanel(GridFilterView)</code>, <code>FindPanel(GridFindView)</code>,
            <code>LoadingIndicator(GridLoadingView)</code>, <code>CellEditor(GridCellEditorView)</code>,
            <code>FormulaBarText(GridFormulaBarTextView)</code>, <code>NameBox(GridNameBoxView)</code> and
            <code>EditorCompletion(GridEditorCompletionView)</code>. A view is
            data, drawn again each time the grid changes it, so the Razor marks its controls with
            <code>ScriptSlotAttributes</code> and handles none of their keys, presses or changes: a menu's control
            for each item carries <code>MenuItem</code> and its index, a filter panel's search box
            <code>FilterSearch</code>, each value's checkbox <code>FilterValue</code> and its index, the Cell Editor's
            one field <code>EditorField</code>, the Formula Bar's <code>FormulaBarField</code> and
            <code>NameBoxField</code>, the completion's list <code>CompletionList</code> and each candidate
            <code>CompletionCandidate</code> and its index, and so on. Which candidate is chosen is the grid's, marked
            <code>aria-selected</code> on your candidate as ↑ and ↓ move it, without drawing the list again, so style
            the chosen one by that attribute. The editor's field is drawn holding the view's
            <code>InitialText</code> and nothing bound after, and the Formula Bar's fields with no value at all: the
            grid reads and writes their text, places the caret and gives them the keyboard in the browser, so what is
            typed is what the Enter commits, and a field a Chrome draws shows a formula's References uncoloured.
            ExSheet therefore keeps its own Cell Editor and Formula Bar text, whatever the Chrome, and takes the
            Chrome's Name Box. On this grid the Name Box names no cell yet, since the grid asks its label in the browser
            and a .NET <code>NameBoxLabel</code> would cost a round trip at every move; Enter in it hands
            <code>OnNameBoxEntered</code> what was typed, and the Example places the Selection on the trade whose Id it
            names. The
            grid's script hands each to the grid in the browser, so nothing a user does waits on a Blazor Server
            circuit, and a key typed as a menu opens, before your Razor has arrived, waits for it and is answered
            there.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>ContextCommands</code> adds your own commands to the Context Menu, after Copy and Copy with
            headers, as on the grid above, and <code>ContextCommandLabel</code> gives them their words (the grid's own commands keep its words). The menu is drawn in
            the browser, so it asks .NET for your commands as it opens and opens once they arrive. A key typed in the
            meantime is held and reaches the menu once it is open. A command's <code>Invoke</code> runs in .NET, and the
            input after it waits until it completes, so the next menu is asked of what the command left. The context's
            <code>Commands</code> is empty here, because Copy runs in the browser. Under React, the same
            <code>contextCommands</code> may answer a promise, and a command's <code>invoke</code> may return one, with
            the same effect. <a href="exchart/grid">Charting a selection</a> uses this.
        </p>
        <p>
            Two rules keep a choice from arriving after the Enter that applies it. A filter or find panel's text
            fields are drawn with no value bound to the view, which arrives a round trip behind the typing and would write an
            older text back; a value field is given the operand it opens with, and nothing after. And a choice is
            made with a control the browser hears: the operators are native selects, as in the React MUI version,
            since a <code>MudSelect</code> tells .NET of a choice only after closing its list, and a condition's
            value is typed as text for every type, read in the column's culture as the grid's own panel reads it.
        </p>
        <p>
            Find is the source's, as on the grid above: <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> asks its
            <code>FindAsync</code> in .NET, and is refused where the source does not search. Replace is not carried
            yet, so <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd> is refused. It takes <code>CellMetrics</code>, and inside
            <code>MudExGridPaper</code> the paper's Roboto widths, Density, hover band and Row Stripes, as the grid
            above does: a value written on the grid beats the paper's.
            A Template Column is drawn as on the grid above: its fragment is rendered in .NET from the row and moved into
            the cell by the grid's script, and <kbd>Space</kbd> asks its control for the keyboard, which it takes
            through <code>HandKeyboardToAsync</code>. The Example's Confirmed column is one. A cell scrolled into view
            shows its content a round trip later over Blazor Server.
            Not carried yet, and refused by name when a column declares it: Tone and Row Marks.
            The Selection Summary is not offered yet either, and no summary is drawn. <code>PointedAt</code> takes
            the declaration a <code>PointingScope</code> made, so a Sheet over the same core points into the grid
            (<a href="exsheet/linked-tables">Linked Tables</a>); a declaration of your own, whose
            <code>OnPress</code> is a .NET function, is refused by name, and <code>OutlinedColumns</code> is not offered yet. Use the grid above for those
            until they arrive. The browser is sent the rows around the ones in view, a few
            screens either side, and asks for more as it scrolls, so a source that holds a million rows sends a
            few hundred. The script it runs is bundled into the package, at an address that changes with its
            content, as ExGrid's own script is, and the core under it is the Shared Script beside it,
            <code>_content/ExGrid/ex-grid-script.min.js</code>, which every product over the core on the page
            shares, so a page with a grid and a pivot loads it once. A Content Security Policy that lists the
            package's files names it too.
        </p>
        <p>
            A dialog or panel of yours that took the keyboard over the grid gives it back with
            <code>ReturnKeyboardAsync</code>, and hands it on to one of your controls with
            <code>HandKeyboardToAsync</code>, as on <code>ExGrid.Components.ExGrid</code>. In the Example, a double
            click on a Trader hands the keyboard from the grid to that trade's note, and Enter there gives it back.
            The overload of <code>ReturnKeyboardAsync</code> that collects the keys typed on a joined surface is not
            carried: this grid joins no surface to its order. Both are judged
            in the browser: a control the user pressed meanwhile keeps the keyboard, however long the circuit took
            to carry the request.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The parts of a grid">
        <p>
            These pages and the API name the parts of a grid as the list under the figure does. Header Groups
            label adjacent columns, and Pinned Columns stay at the edge while the others pan under them. The Mark
            Column's ticks are Row Marks, which your application holds. A Selection is rectangles of cells, as in
            Excel: the Focus is the cell you are on, and the Extent is the end that moves as you extend the range.
            Under the grid, the Selection Summary gives Excel's status-bar figures over it. A number too wide for
            its column shows <code>####</code>, never a shorter number.
        </p>
        <DocsFigure Name="grid-anatomy">
            Positions under three Header Groups, panned until P&amp;L shows whole, with two rows marked and a range selected.
            A script draws the figure from <a href="figure/grid-anatomy" target="_blank">the grid on a page of its own</a>.
        </DocsFigure>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="The grid neither holds nor executes">
        <p>
            ExGrid paints the rows it is given and reports what the user does. It does not own the data, and it does
            not sort, filter, search or write anything itself:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>A <strong>header click</strong> asks for a sort. A <strong>filter panel</strong> asks for a filter. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> asks where the next match is.</li>
            <li>The <strong>status-bar figures</strong> — Excel's Average, Count and Sum over the selection — are asked of whoever holds the rows, for every selected row.</li>
            <li>A <strong>commit in the editor</strong>, a <strong>paste</strong> and a <strong>Delete</strong> each arrive as an intent — "this row, this column, this value" — that your application applies, or not.</li>
            <li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> is forwarded to you, because only your application sees every change.</li>
            <li>Column widths, order and visibility are your state too: a drag reports the new layout, and you hand back a new column list.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            That keeps one source of truth — yours — and lets the same grid sit over a list in memory, a database or a
            live feed.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A Source, or a Window you push">
        <p>
            There are two ways to give the grid rows. A <strong>Source</strong> —
            <code>GridSource.From(rows)</code> for a list in memory, <code>GridSource.Fetch(...)</code> for a server —
            holds the rows and answers the grid's questions for you. Or you push a <strong>Window</strong> yourself:
            the rows you have, where they start and how many there are, answering <code>OnRangeNeeded</code> when the
            user scrolls past them. A Source drives that same push form, so the grid behaves identically either way.
            Either way it paints only the rows in view, on both axes: a million rows and a hundred columns keep the same
            few dozen elements. See <a href="exgrid/data">Data sources</a>.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="It refuses rather than guesses">
        <p>
            ExGrid was built for money and risk numbers, where a plausible wrong answer is worse than none. Where it
            cannot do something correctly, it says so:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>A number too wide for its column shows <code>####</code>, never a truncated figure that reads as a smaller one.</li>
            <li>A copy larger than the cap copies <strong>nothing</strong>, rather than the first rows with a total quietly too small.</li>
            <li>A paste never writes outside the selection; a block pasted onto one cell is refused.</li>
            <li>When the order of the rows changes, or another <code>Source</code> is handed over, the selection is dropped rather than left on rows nobody chose, and a write aimed before it is refused rather than landing on another row.</li>
            <li>The status-bar figures belong to the selection standing now: a new selection clears them at once, and a total is never shown for part of what was selected.</li>
            <li>Where nothing can search every row, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd> is refused rather than left to the browser, which sees only the painted rows.</li>
            <li>A malformed filter, an unknown column or a contradictory parameter throws, naming what was wrong.</li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Two rules that keep it fast">
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>A row repaints when its instance changes.</strong> Rows skip their render while their instance
                is the same, so rewriting a row object in place does not repaint it. Replace the instance instead —
                records and <code>with</code> make it one line.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Give the grid the same objects across renders.</strong> Keep the source, the column list and
                any delegates you pass in fields. A new array or lambda on every render looks like a change and renders
                every row again; handed nothing new, the grid does not render at all, so restyling the element around it
                costs no render.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

